---
// src/pages/bucknell/anop-400.astro  →  arizvi.com/bucknell/anop-400
// ANOP 400: how the term runs, where the clients come from, how it's graded, and what happens when it goes wrong. Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import CourseHeader from '../../components/CourseHeader.astro';
import CourseNav from '../../components/CourseNav.astro';
import Gantt from '../../components/Gantt.astro';
import { course } from '../../data/bucknell';

const c = course('anop-400');

// The ANOP 400 term, week by week.
const term = [
  { label: 'Scope', bars: [[1, 3]] as [number, number][] },
  { label: 'Data and discovery', bars: [[4, 6]] as [number, number][] },
  { label: 'Modelling', bars: [[7, 10]] as [number, number][] },
  { label: 'Recommendation', bars: [[11, 13]] as [number, number][] },
  { label: 'Delivery', bars: [[14, 14]] as [number, number][] },
  { label: 'With the client', marks: [{ w: 1 }, { w: 3 }, { w: 8 }, { w: 11 }, { w: 14 }] },
  { label: 'Team stand-ups', ticks: Array.from({ length: 14 }, (_, i) => i + 1) },
];

const brief = [
  ['The client', 'A regional credit union with 9 branches. Contact: the head of lending.'],
  ['The decision', 'Which members to call in the month before their car loan ends, so they refinance here.'],
  ['The data', '3 years of auto loans, about 40,000 rows. Member IDs replaced before the file reaches the team.'],
  ['The deliverable', 'A ranked call list for one month, and a 2-page memo on what a call is worth and where the cutoff sits.'],
  ['Success', 'A backtest on the last 6 months: how many of the members who refinanced elsewhere the list would have flagged in time.'],
  ['The client gives', '5 meetings (weeks 1, 3, 8, 11 and 14), a named contact, and the data by week 4.'],
];

const grading = [
  ['Charter', '10%', 'Week 3. Signed by the client.'],
  ['Mid-term check-in', '15%', 'Week 8. The data audit and first findings, presented to the client.'],
  ['Final presentation and report', '45%', 'Week 14. The client in the room, and the model attached.'],
  ['Stand-ups and peer evaluation', '20%', 'Every week. Peer evaluation at week 7 and week 14 moves a team mark up or down for each member.'],
  ['Reflection', '10%', 'Week 14. 2 pages: what you would do differently, and what you would tell next year\'s team.'],
];
---
<Base title="ANOP 400" description="ANOP 400, the Business Analytics Practicum, as Ali Rizvi would run it at Bucknell: the term, the clients, the grading, and what happens when a project goes wrong." noindex>
  <CourseHeader course={c} facts={[
    ['Format', '1 credit · 14 weeks · teams of 4 or 5, a client each'],
    ['Grading', 'Charter 10% · mid-term check-in 15% · final presentation and report 45% · stand-ups and peer evaluation 20% · reflection 10%'],
    ['AI tools', 'Allowed on public data and on your own writing. Nothing from a client file goes into a chatbot unless the data agreement says it can.'],
    ['Track record', "I ran Waterloo's Undergraduate Capstone Seminar the same way, with external partners every term. There the teams built working prototypes. Here the deliverable is a model and a recommendation the client can act on."],
  ]}>
    <p>ANOP 400 has a client pipeline and an instructor who runs it, and I'd learn it before I touched it: sit in on the spring 2028 run, take a team if that helps, and teach a section when the department asks. Below is how I ran a capstone at Waterloo, translated to this one.</p>
    <p class="practice"><span class="label">From practice</span>A clinical trial is a capstone with higher stakes: the protocol is written before the first patient, the success measure is named in advance, and nobody moves it. The A/B tests I ran inside an e-commerce retailer worked the same way, with the metric and the stopping rule agreed before launch, and the ones that skipped that step produced quick wins that never showed up in revenue. The charter above is that discipline, scaled to 14 weeks.</p>
  </CourseHeader>

  <Section label="The term" title="Week by week" id="term">
    <Gantt title="The ANOP 400 term, week by week" weeks={14} rows={term}
      caption="With the client: kick-off (week 1), signed charter (3), check-in (8), draft recommendation (11), final presentation (14). Every team gets a 15-minute stand-up with me each week." />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Clients" title="Where the projects come from" id="clients">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Sourcing starts the term before, so every team has a client in week 1 and a signed charter by week 3. The pool: businesses within an hour of campus, the small businesses that come through Bucknell's SBDC, alumni-run companies, and regional non-profits with data they have never looked at. A company I or AMJ Capital hold a stake in is never a client of a team I grade.</p>
      <p>Each client signs the 1-page charter and a data agreement with it: what the team gets, who may see it, and that the client keeps the data and the model. Customer and member IDs are replaced before a file reaches students, and students sign an NDA where the client asks for one.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top: 2rem;"><div class="span-8">
      <p class="label">A sample brief · fictional</p>
      <div class="kv brief">
        {brief.map(([k, v]) => (<><p class="label">{k}</p><p>{v}</p></>))}
      </div>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Grading" title="What the mark is made of" id="grading">
    <ul class="rows compact bk-wide">
      {grading.map(([item, weight, note]) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{weight}</div>
          <div class="body"><span class="title">{item}</span><p>{note}</p></div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="When it goes wrong" title="The week 9 playbook" id="playbook">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The client goes quiet.</strong> It happens, and usually around week 9. The charter names a plan B dataset in the same domain, and the team switches at that week's stand-up without losing the week.</li>
        <li><strong>The data is late.</strong> The week 4 deadline is in the charter. The team starts on a public stand-in the same day, and the client's file replaces it when it lands.</li>
        <li><strong>A teammate disappears.</strong> Stand-up records and the week 7 peer evaluation make it visible early. I have the conversation in week 7, and the grade follows the record.</li>
        <li><strong>The model says nothing useful.</strong> That is a finding. The memo says what the data can and cannot support, and what to collect next year.</li>
      </ol>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <CourseNav current={c.slug} />
</Base>
